---

import Button from './Button.astro';
import StatRow from './StatRow.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/site';
---

<section class="relative z-0 overflow-hidden">
  <!-- `z-0` matters, not just cosmetic: `position:relative` alone doesn't create a
       stacking context without an explicit z-index, so the negative-z layers below
       would otherwise escape it and paint behind the page's own background instead
       of just behind this section's content — confirmed by direct DOM inspection,
       2026-07-28. This was silently hiding the photo below entirely. -->

  <!--
    Full-bleed hero photo, mirrors design/mockups/Screenshot 2026-07-21 112341.png:
    a background layer behind the copy, not a boxed/bordered image. Full-width behind
    the copy below lg — a near-opaque wash there, so the mobile view reads as faint
    texture, not a distinct photo (human review, 2026-07-28). From lg, narrows to the
    right 62% with the left/bottom fades below, so the photo reads clearly there,
    matching the mockup. Two gradient divs (not the photo itself) fade it into navy-950.

    ASSET: cnc-machine.png (design/visual-assets/hero-page-cnc.png), swapped in
    2026-07-31 for a whole, mid-operation CNC milling machine — replaces the earlier
    macro-gears close-up (design/visual-assets/gears.png), which moved to the
    Try It Yourself subpage instead (see try-it-yourself.astro). Reasoning:
    macro-gears is generic "industrial" stock imagery that could be almost any
    manufacturing product; a real CNC mill visually grounds the actual product
    (docs/IMAGE_ASSETS.md item 1).

    Container shape, not just the source file, changed with it: the old portrait
    macro-gears photo (1600×2000) suited `inset-0` stretched to the section's full
    (tall) height. This new photo is landscape (1672×941, ~16:9) with intentional
    dark negative space on the left for the headline to sit on — stretching THAT to
    a tall container would object-cover-crop away most of its width, defeating the
    wide composition entirely. `lg:aspect-video` (an exact match for the source's
    own ~16:9 ratio) plus vertical centering keeps the photo's real shape intact
    regardless of how tall the copy block next to it renders.
  -->
  <div
    class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-20 lg:inset-auto lg:right-0 lg:top-1/2 lg:w-[62%] lg:aspect-video lg:-translate-y-1/2"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
    <img
      src={withBase('/images/hero/cnc-machine.png')}
      alt=""
      class="h-full w-full object-cover"
    />
    <!-- near-opaque wash below lg — the photo is felt, not seen, on narrow screens -->
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-navy-950/85 lg:hidden"></div>
    <!-- fade into the page background on the left edge (desktop only) -->
    <div
      class="absolute inset-0 hidden lg:block"
      style="background: linear-gradient(to right, var(--color-navy-950) 0%, transparent 42%);"
    >
    </div>
    <!-- fade into the page background at the bottom edge -->
    <div
      class="absolute inset-0"
      style="background: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 55%, var(--color-navy-950) 100%);"
    >
    </div>

    <!-- Failure-mode hotspots (human-requested, 2026-07-31) — restrained version of
         LiveFactory.astro's pin-overlay pattern: icons only, no text/labels baked
         into the image or drawn here (gpt-image-2 can't render small text reliably,
         and acronyms like "TWF" would need explaining before a first-time visitor
         has any context — the Krug/Redish plain-language problem this site
         otherwise solves with InfoTip). Three of the dataset's real failure
         classes; still no hover card and still lg-only (this photo is just a
         felt-not-seen wash below lg, so a pinned icon would be nearly invisible
         there) — that restraint stands. RNF (random failure) has no icon on
         purpose: it has no predictable sensor signature by definition in the
         dataset, so implying this system flags it too would overclaim.
         Coordinates are eyeballed off cnc-machine.png and expected to need a small
         nudge once seen rendered — same as LiveFactory's own pin coordinates.

         Visibility pass (human-requested follow-up, 2026-07-31): the first version
         was too subtle to notice — a soft blurred wash, breathing over 10-13s, with
         no arrival moment and nothing to distinguish it from the photo's own
         highlights/sparks. Fixed three ways (`.hero-icon-marker` below), all still
         non-interactive/pointer-events-none — this remains a flourish, not
         LiveFactory's "watch it work" moment, just a more noticeable one:
         (A) a visible ring + a tighter, brighter two-layer glow (teal core, dark
         vignette behind it) reading as a deliberate marker against the photo's own
         texture, and a faster 6-7s breathe instead of 10-13s; (B) a one-time
         brightness flash timed just after the copy's own hero-fade-in sequence
         finishes (no scroll/JS trigger needed — the hero's already in view at
         load); (D) the dark vignette IS the local-contrast patch each glow sits
         on, folded into the same background rather than a separate layer. -->
    <div
      class="hero-icon-marker absolute hidden -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full border border-teal-400/45 p-2.5 lg:block"
      style="left: 60%; top: 46%; animation-duration: 6.5s, 900ms; animation-delay: 0ms, 480ms; background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(45,212,191,0.55), transparent 55%), radial-gradient(circle, rgba(13,27,46,0.6), transparent 80%); box-shadow: 0 0 18px 5px rgba(45,212,191,0.4);"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      <svg width="30" height="24" viewBox="0 0 30 24" fill="none">
        <path d="M11.5 5.6a4.5 4.5 0 0 0-6 5.8L1 16v3h3l4.6-4.5a4.5 4.5 0 0 0 5.8-6l-2.7 2.7-2-2 2.7-2.7Z" stroke="var(--color-teal-400)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <rect x="21" y="4" width="3" height="18" rx="1" fill="var(--color-teal-400)" opacity="0.2" />
        <rect x="21" y="16" width="3" height="6" rx="1" fill="var(--color-teal-400)" />
        <rect x="26" y="4" width="3" height="18" rx="1" fill="var(--color-teal-400)" opacity="0.2" />
        <rect x="26" y="18" width="3" height="4" rx="1" fill="var(--color-teal-400)" />
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div
      class="hero-icon-marker absolute hidden -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full border border-teal-400/45 p-2.5 lg:block"
      style="left: 73%; top: 46%; animation-duration: 7s, 900ms; animation-delay: -2.3s, 560ms; background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(45,212,191,0.55), transparent 55%), radial-gradient(circle, rgba(13,27,46,0.6), transparent 80%); box-shadow: 0 0 18px 5px rgba(45,212,191,0.4);"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      <svg width="23" height="23" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
        <rect x="2" y="8" width="17" height="8" rx="1.5" stroke="var(--color-teal-400)" stroke-width="1.6" />
        <path d="M21 10.5v3" stroke="var(--color-teal-400)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <rect x="4" y="10" width="3" height="4" rx="0.5" fill="var(--color-teal-400)" />
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div
      class="hero-icon-marker absolute hidden -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 rounded-full border border-teal-400/45 p-2.5 lg:block"
      style="left: 93%; top: 48%; animation-duration: 6.8s, 900ms; animation-delay: -4.6s, 640ms; background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(45,212,191,0.55), transparent 55%), radial-gradient(circle, rgba(13,27,46,0.6), transparent 80%); box-shadow: 0 0 18px 5px rgba(45,212,191,0.4);"
      aria-hidden="true"
    >
      <svg width="23" height="23" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none">
        <path d="M14 14.76V4.5a2 2 0 0 0-4 0v10.26a3.5 3.5 0 1 0 4 0Z" stroke="var(--color-teal-400)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round" />
        <path d="M12 7v7.5" stroke="var(--color-teal-400)" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" />
        <circle cx="12" cy="17" r="1.8" fill="var(--color-teal-400)" />
      </svg>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Cyberpunk glow overlay (separate from the photo, per §5). Two layers,
       each breathing on its own slow, offset cycle (ambient-breathe,
       global.css) instead of one static combined background — reads as
       ambient light drifting, not a single uniform pulse (human-requested,
       2026-07-28: ambient sophistication for the page's first impression). -->
  <div
    class="hero-glow pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10"
    style="background: radial-gradient(60% 55% at 78% 35%, rgba(45,212,191,0.16), transparent 70%); animation-duration: 11s;"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
  </div>
  <div
    class="hero-glow pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10"
    style="background: radial-gradient(45% 40% at 12% 8%, rgba(59,130,246,0.14), transparent 70%); animation-duration: 15s; animation-delay: -6s;"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
  </div>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-5 py-16 md:px-8 md:py-24">
    <!-- Copy block. Above-the-fold and already in view at first paint, so this
         uses a load-triggered staggered fade-up (.hero-fade-in) rather than the
         scroll-triggered [data-reveal] mechanism used everywhere below the fold —
         there's nothing to "scroll into view" here. -->
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <p
        class="hero-fade-in mb-5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-blue-500"
        style="animation-delay: 0ms"
      >
        Predictive maintenance, powered by AI
      </p>

      <h1
        class="hero-fade-in text-5xl font-bold leading-[1.05] tracking-tight sm:text-6xl 2xl:text-7xl"
        style="animation-delay: 60ms"
      >
        <span class="text-offwhite">Predict failures.</span><br />
        <span class="text-teal-400">Prevent</span> <span class="text-offwhite">downtime.</span>
      </h1>

      <p
        class="hero-fade-in mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted 2xl:max-w-2xl 2xl:text-xl"
        style="animation-delay: 140ms"
      >
        A predictive-maintenance system that catches machine failures before
        they happen — built to show how manufacturing teams could maximize
        uptime and efficiency.
      </p>

      <div class="hero-fade-in mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-4" style="animation-delay: 220ms">
        <Button href={withBase('/product')} variant="solid">See it in action →</Button>
        <Button href={withBase('/try-it-yourself')} variant="outline">
          Test it on your device
        </Button>
      </div>

      <p class="hero-fade-in mt-5 text-xs text-muted/60" style="animation-delay: 280ms">
        A capstone project showcase, running on simulated CNC sensor data.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Stat row sits on the dark/faded area, wider than the copy block, as in the mockup -->
    <div class="hero-fade-in mt-10 max-w-4xl" style="animation-delay: 340ms">
      <StatRow />
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Failure-mode icon markers (see the markup comment above for the full
     visibility-pass rationale). Two animations per marker, same technique as
     LiveFactory's .sensor-pin: the continuous ambient-breathe (shared
     keyframe, global.css) plus a one-time brightness flash. Unlike
     LiveFactory's boot-flash — gated behind an IntersectionObserver +
     .is-visible class, since that section only enters view on scroll — this
     needs no JS trigger at all: the hero is already in view at first paint,
     so the flash just rides a plain positive animation-delay, timed to land
     right after .hero-fade-in's own copy sequence (which ends at 340ms)
     finishes, drawing the eye across to the photo once the headline/CTAs
     have already appeared. animation-duration/-delay are set inline per
     marker as comma-separated pairs (ambient-breathe value, then
     hero-icon-flash value) so each marker's continuous breathe stays
     independently offset — three markers pulsing in lockstep would read as
     one fake blinking light — while its one-time flash still lands at its
     own distinct moment. */
  .hero-icon-marker {
    animation: ambient-breathe 7s ease-in-out infinite, hero-icon-flash 900ms ease-out both;
  }
  @keyframes hero-icon-flash {
    0% { filter: brightness(1); }
    35% { filter: brightness(2.4); }
    100% { filter: brightness(1); }
  }
</style>
